To represent your measurement of the yield strength of a copper rod from a tensile test, you can use the class:
Lower Yield Strength (IRI: https://w3id.org/steel/ProcessOntology/LowerYieldStrength)
This class represents the yield strength of a material, the stress point at which the material transitions from elastic to plastic deformation. It is typically measured in Pascals (Pa), which fits your 210 MPa measurement.
To connect this measurement to the specimen, you can use properties related to the specimen such as specimen geometry or dimensions. For example, specimen width can be represented by the class:
Specimen Width (IRI: https://w3id.org/steel/ProcessOntology/SpecimenWidth)
There is a property to link specimen width:
hasSpecimenWidth (IRI: https://w3id.org/steel/ProcessOntology/hasSpecimenWidth_Object) which links an entity (e.g., a test piece or specimen) to its width.
For the measurement process and linking the measurement to the specimen, you can use measurement-related classes and properties:
Measurement (IRI: https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_463bcfda_867b_41d9_a967_211d4d437cfb) - represents the measurement activity.
The property "Has Measured Property" (IRI: https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_fd689787_31b0_41cf_bf03_0d69af76469d) assigns a quantity to an object via a measurement procedure.
The property "is quality measurement of" (IRI: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000221) links a measurement datum to the quality measured, which could be the specimen or material.
The property "Has Measuring Device" (IRI: https://w3id.org/steel/ProcessOntology/hasMeasuringDevice) can be used to indicate the instrument used for the test.
Example RDF Turtle snippet illustrating how the yield strength measurement might be described and linked to the specimen:
turtle

@prefix steel: <https://w3id.org/steel/ProcessOntology/> .
@prefix emmo: <https://w3id.org/emmo#> .
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/> .

ex:copperRod a steel:TestPieceGeometry ;
    steel:hasSpecimenWidth ex:widthValue .

ex:yieldStrengthMeasurement a steel:LowerYieldStrength ;
    emmo:HasMeasuredProperty ex:copperRod ;
    steel:hasMeasuringDevice ex:tensileTestMachine ;
    emmo:measurementValue "210"^^xsd:decimal ;
    emmo:measurementUnit "MPa" .

ex:tensileTestMachine a emmo:MeasuringInstrument .
This setup uses the class for Lower Yield Strength and links it with the test specimen and the measurement process. If you want, I can help you generate a more detailed and aligned example based on your exact data and experimental setup.

To represent your steel sample being heat-treated and quenched, you can use these process classes from ontologies:
Heat Treatment:
From PMD Core Ontology: https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0000779 This class represents a process modifying material properties through time-temperature sequences without changing the shape.
From EMMO: https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_dacfc7dc_5ddb_4f67_986b_dcd01d649d60 This is under MaterialTreatment and denotes processing to transform material structure by any treatment excluding synthesis.
Quenching:
From KupferDigital Copper Ontology: https://kupferdigital.gitlab.io/ontologies/kdo/Quenching This class represents the quenching process (rapid cooling).
You can model your experiment as a sequence of these processes: a Heat Treatment with parameters (temperature 850°C, duration 2 hours), followed by a Quenching process, e.g., rapid cooling in water.
Example RDF snippet (Turtle) to express this might be:
ttl

@prefix pmd: <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/> .
@prefix emmo: <https://w3id.org/emmo#> .
@prefix kdo: <https://kupferdigital.gitlab.io/ontologies/kdo/> .
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

ex:heatTreatment1 a pmd:PMD_0000779 ;  # Heat treatment
    ex:temperature "850"^^xsd:decimal ; 
    ex:temperatureUnit "°C" ;
    ex:duration "PT2H"^^xsd:duration . # ISO 8601 duration for 2 hours

ex:quenching1 a kdo:Quenching ;
    ex:coolingMedium ex:Water .

ex:experiment1 a emmo:EMMO_dacfc7dc_5ddb_4f67_986b_dcd01d649d60 ; # MaterialTreatment
    emmo:hasPartProcess ex:heatTreatment1, ex:quenching1 .
This structure links the processes sequentially as parts of an overall material treatment experiment. If you want, I can assist defining properties like temperature, duration, and cooling medium from available ontologies or suggest best practice vocabularies to describe these details. Would you like that?

To describe the chemical composition of a 316L stainless steel alloy in RDF using the available ontologies, you can start by modeling the steel as an instance of the stainless steel class (https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0010003).
Then, you represent the alloying elements as proportions of pure chemical elements:
Portion of chromium (https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020029) for the 16–18% Cr content,
Portion of nickel (https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020051) for the 10–14% Ni content,
Portion of manganese (https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0020078) for the max 2% Mn content.
Each portion of pure chemical element is a pure substance composed of atoms of the same element.
You would then link these portions to the stainless steel alloy instance using appropriate properties (e.g., composition, hasPart, or other relevant properties you find in the ontology for material composition).
Here is a simplified RDF Turtle example snippet illustrating this concept:
ttl

@prefix pmd: <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/> .
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/materials/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

ex:Steel316L a pmd:PMD_0010003 ;  # stainless steel
    pmd:hasPart [
        a pmd:PMD_0020029 ;  # portion of chromium
        pmd:percentage "16"^^xsd:decimal
    ] ;
    pmd:hasPart [
        a pmd:PMD_0020029 ;  # portion of chromium
        pmd:percentage "18"^^xsd:decimal
    ] ;
    pmd:hasPart [
        a pmd:PMD_0020051 ;  # portion of nickel
        pmd:percentage "10"^^xsd:decimal
    ] ;
    pmd:hasPart [
        a pmd:PMD_0020051 ;  # portion of nickel
        pmd:percentage "14"^^xsd:decimal
    ] ;
    pmd:hasPart [
        a pmd:PMD_0020078 ;  # portion of manganese
        pmd:percentage "2"^^xsd:decimal
    ] .
