In the EMMO ontology, the class "Measurement" (https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_463bcfda_867b_41d9_a967_211d4d437cfb) is defined as a subclass of "Procedure" and "Observation." It is described as an intentional process where an agent works with some entities according to existing formalized rules and implies a causal interaction between the object and the observer.
The class "Observation" (https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_3b19eab4_79be_4b02_bdaf_ecf1f0067a68) is a subclass of "Determination" and is characterized as a description of an object with an actual interaction. It involves an observer perceiving a physical object through a perception mechanism and producing a property as a result of this perception according to a defined procedure.
For a scanning electron microscope (SEM) observation, you are indeed characterizing an object with an actual interaction (electron beam interaction with the sample), and there is a defined procedure that produces quantitative or qualitative results.
Therefore, "Measurement" would be the more precise class to use for SEM observation because it specifically involves an intentional process to obtain measurable results through a causal interaction with the object, which fits the nature of SEM observations.
Usage example in TTL could be:
ttl

@prefix emmo: <https://w3id.org/emmo#> .
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/> .

ex:SEMObservation a emmo:Measurement ;
  rdfs:label "SEM Observation of Sample X" ;
  emmo:hasInstrument ex:ScanningElectronMicroscope ;
  emmo:targetsObject ex:SampleX .
Here, "ex
" is an instance of the emmo
 class indicating a scanning electron microscope observation procedure.

To express that two different labs measured the same property on the same batch of material but obtained slightly different values, you can use the following concepts from EMMO and the Steel Process Ontology:
Measurement (EMMO): https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_463bcfda_867b_41d9_a967_211d4d437cfb
Defined as an intentional process involving an interaction between an object and an observer to obtain measurement results.
It is a subclass of Procedure and Observation.
Measurement Result (EMMO): https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0f6f0120_c079_4d95_bb11_4ddee05e530e
Represents the result of a measurement, including quantities attributed to a measurand together with relevant information like measurement uncertainty.
Measurer (EMMO): https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_9be5fcc4_0d8b_481d_b984_6338d4b55588
An observer that uses a measurement tool and provides a quantitative property.
Laboratory (Steel Process Ontology): https://w3id.org/steel/ProcessOntology/Laboratory
Represents a laboratory entity where measurement experiments are conducted.
You can model the provenance of measurements from different labs by creating Measurement instances for each lab's experiment, linking each Measurement to the same material batch (as object), specifying each lab as the Measurer or associated Laboratory, and recording the Measurement Result including uncertainties.
For example, in Turtle syntax:
ttl

@prefix emmo: <https://w3id.org/emmo#> .
@prefix steel: <https://w3id.org/steel/ProcessOntology/> .
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/> .

# The material batch
ex:MaterialBatch1 a emmo:Material .

# Measurement by Lab1
ex:MeasurementLab1 a emmo:Measurement ;
    emmo:hasMeasurer ex:Lab1 ;
    emmo:hasMeasuredObject ex:MaterialBatch1 ;
    emmo:hasMeasurementResult ex:ResultLab1 .

ex:Lab1 a steel:Laboratory .

ex:ResultLab1 a emmo:MeasurementResult ;
    emmo:hasQuantityValue "Value1"^^xsd:float ;
    emmo:hasMeasurementUncertainty "Uncertainty1"^^xsd:float .

# Measurement by Lab2
ex:MeasurementLab2 a emmo:Measurement ;
    emmo:hasMeasurer ex:Lab2 ;
    emmo:hasMeasuredObject ex:MaterialBatch1 ;
    emmo:hasMeasurementResult ex:ResultLab2 .

ex:Lab2 a steel:Laboratory .

ex:ResultLab2 a emmo:MeasurementResult ;
    emmo:hasQuantityValue "Value2"^^xsd:float ;
    emmo:hasMeasurementUncertainty "Uncertainty2"^^xsd:float .
This way, you explicitly represent measurement provenance and differing results from inter-lab measurements on the same material batch. Would you like me to check if there are specific properties to link a Measurement to a Laboratory or Measurer in these ontologies?

ou can chain the processes in your experiment using properties from the EMMO ontology that relate processes and their inputs/outputs.
The main relevant properties are:
emmo
(https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_36e69413_8c59_4799_946c_10b05d266e22) which relates a process to its input entity.
emmo
(https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c4bace1d_4db0_4cd3_87e9_18122bae2840) which relates a process to its output entity.
emmo
(https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_eaef29bd_bd79_4de1_b192_4fdc9016730b) which relates one process as the input of another process.
Your processes from the KDO ontology are subclasses of ManufacturingProcess or Rolling and Annealing processes. You can model each step as an instance of these process classes and use these properties to chain them by stating that the output material of one process is the input material of the next process.
Example in TTL (using example IRIs for processes and materials):
turtle

@prefix emmo: <https://w3id.org/emmo#> .
@prefix kdo: <https://kupferdigital.gitlab.io/ontologies/kdo/> .

# Instances of the processes
:castingProcess a kdo:Casting ;
    emmo:HasOutput :materialAfterCasting .

:homogenizationAnnealingProcess a kdo:HomogenizationAnnealing ;
    emmo:HasInput :materialAfterCasting ;
    emmo:HasOutput :materialAfterHomogenization .

:coldRollingProcess a kdo:ColdRolling ;
    emmo:HasInput :materialAfterHomogenization ;
    emmo:HasOutput :materialAfterColdRolling .

:recrystallizationAnnealingProcess a kdo:HomogenizationAnnealing ; # or a specific class for recrystallization annealing if exists
    emmo:HasInput :materialAfterColdRolling ;
    emmo:HasOutput :finalMaterial .
This way, you explicitly represent the chaining of processes by linking outputs of one process as inputs to the next. Let me know if you want examples using PMDCore instead or details about any specific part.

To semantically distinguish between 'nominal composition' (what you intended) and 'actual composition' (measured by EDS), here are relevant ontology concepts and patterns:
Composition as an intensive quality:
The PMD Core Ontology defines "composition" (https://w3id.org/pmd/co/PMD_0025001) as an intensive quality that defines the types and proportions of compounds present in a material entity. This can represent the actual or intended composition of a sample.
This "composition" is independent of the size of the portion of matter.
Nominal Property for intended values:
The EMMO ontology defines "Nominal Property" (https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_909415d1_7c43_4d5e_bbeb_7e1910159f66) as a property of a phenomenon, body, or substance where the property has no magnitude but has a value expressed in words, codes, or other means.
This can be used to represent the "nominal composition," i.e., the intended or designed composition as coded or stated conventionally.
Actual composition as measured quality value:
The actual composition determined by measurement methods like EDS can be an instance of the "composition" quality, with data items or measurement results linked.
Measurement devices and processes are modeled in PMD ontology (e.g., spectrometer, spectroscopy methods) to provide context on how this quality was obtained.
Suggested usage pattern in RDF Turtle:
ttl

@prefix pmd: <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/> .
@prefix emmo: <https://w3id.org/emmo#> .
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/> .

ex:Sample1 a pmd:MaterialEntity ;
   pmd:hasNominalComposition ex:NominalComposition1 ;
   pmd:hasActualComposition ex:ActualComposition1 .

ex:NominalComposition1 a emmo:NominalProperty ;
   emmo:hasValue "Al 4.5%, Cu 1.0%, Mg 0.5%" .

ex:ActualComposition1 a pmd:Composition ;
   pmd:compositionDataItem ex:EDSDataItem1 .

ex:EDSDataItem1 a pmd:ChemicalCompositionDataItem ;
   pmd:measuredBy ex:EDSDevice1 ;
   pmd:fractionValueSpecification [
       pmd:chemicalElement "Al" ;
       pmd:fractionValue "4.3"^^xsd:decimal
   ] .
This approach uses:
emmo
for the intended composition (nominal composition).
pmd
with data items for the actual composition measured.
Links to measurement devices or techniques for provenance.
