To describe your measurement of electrical resistivity in μΩ·cm in RDF using QUDT and EMMO ontologies, you can link your specimen, the property (electrical resistivity), the unit (microohm centimeter), and the numeric value as follows:
Use the EMMO class for Electrical Resistivity:

IRI: https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_e150fa8d_06dc_4bb8_bf95_04e2aea529c1

(This class is a derived quantity under ISQDerivedQuantity and ElectromagneticQuantity.)
Use the QUDT unit for MicroOhmCentimeter:

IRI: http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/MicroOhmCentimeter
Express the numeric value as a literal (e.g., "5.0"^^xsd
).
Link these elements using properties that relate the specimen to its property, the property to its quantity value, and the quantity value to the unit.
Here is an example RDF Turtle snippet illustrating this:
ttl

@prefix ex: <http://example.org/your#> .
@prefix emmo: <https://w3id.org/emmo#> .
@prefix qudt-unit: <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/> .
@prefix qudt: <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

ex:specimen123 a ex:Material ;  # Your specimen
  ex:hasProperty ex:electricalResistivityMeasurement .

ex:electricalResistivityMeasurement a emmo:EMMO_e150fa8d_06dc_4bb8_bf95_04e2aea529c1 ; # ElectricalResistivity
  qudt:quantityValue ex:value123 .

ex:value123 a qudt:QuantityValue ;
  qudt:numericValue "5.0"^^xsd:double ;
  qudt:unit qudt-unit:MicroOhmCentimeter .
In this example:
ex:specimen123 is your specimen individual.
ex:hasProperty (you might need to choose or define an appropriate property relating specimen to property based on the ontology you use).
The property instance ex:electricalResistivityMeasurement is typed as Electrical Resistivity from EMMO.
The actual measurement value is linked via qudt:quantityValue to a qudt:QuantityValue instance specifying the numeric value and unit.
